{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305211,"nonce":"iGVDq9NTDuMdIkmp+KGFELRPZSA=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPiJYw","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/28","origin":"github"},"title":"Tracking issue: rewrite the core around a global piece model","message":"Most of the correctness bugs in this tracker share one root cause: the tool is built around \"for each torrent file, find same-sized candidates and check that file in isolation\". That model cannot represent a piece that spans two files, which is why boundary pieces never verify and why the merge path miscomputes offsets.\n\nThe alternative is to model the torrent the way the format actually defines it: **one global byte stream**, the concatenation of all files, cut into fixed-size pieces. Recovery becomes \"for each piece index, find a source that produces bytes matching the expected digest\".\n\nA *layout* binds torrent-file slots to concrete paths on disk. A piece is verifiable against a layout when every slot it touches is bound. Spanning pieces then work by construction rather than needing a special case, and a layout with only one slot bound is just a degenerate layout — so loose size-matching and structured directory-matching become the same code path.\n\nProposed shape:\n\n```\ndiscovery  -\u003e  verification  -\u003e  planning  -\u003e  recovery\nwalk fs        hash pieces       assign        write\nbuild index    per source        pieces to     (verify\nbuild layouts  (read only)       sources        on write)\n```\n\nPlanning as a pure function of the verification report means it is testable with no I/O and serialisable, so a plan can be reviewed before anything is written.\n\nCloses, structurally rather than individually, the issues covering spanning pieces, merge offsets, zero-length files, final partial pieces and padding files.\n\nSuggested order: read-only core (parse, offset model, scan, verify) first, then the write path, then concurrency. The single-threaded rewrite already removes the algorithmic overhead; concurrency adds maybe 2-4x on top and only on fast storage.","files":null}]}